// sigLock unlocks an output to the holder of the ed25519 keys whose blake2b
// hash equals the holderID stored at position 0 of the index-value tuple
// (output element index 1).
//
// The public lock symbol `sigLock` is a 0-arg constraint — its bytecode at
// the lock element (output element index 2) carries no data. The holderID
// lives only in the index-value tuple and is read via `selfIndexValue(0)`
// by the wrapper. The underscored primitive `_sigLock` keeps the original
// validation logic and takes the holderID as $0.

// Compares two consumed inputs to decide whether one references the other for
// unlock. BOTH must carry the plain sigLock at the lock element AND the same
// holderID at position 0 of the index-value tuple.
//
// The lock-type check is load-bearing, not a shape assertion. The shortcut
// infers "the referenced input validated with my lock and my holder, so my
// holder signed", which only follows when that lock has a single consume
// path. Conditional locks have several — public cleanup, target accept, chain
// unlock — so a referenced input of that kind proves nothing about its
// holder's consent, and on the sendWithDeadline target path position 0 is not
// even the party being authorised. Keeping the check here rather than at the
// call site covers both ways those locks arrive: `_sigLock` and, for the dex
// orders' reclaim window, the public `sigLock` wrapper.
//
// $0 — referenced input index, 1 byte
func unlockedByReference: and(
    equal(len($0), u64/1),
	lessThan($0, selfOutputIndex),                                       // strictly smaller index, no cycles
	selfHasLockType(#sigLock),                                           // my own lock is the plain sigLock
	equal(self, consumedConstraintByIndex($0, lockConstraintIndex)),     // same lock kind (both sigLock)
	equal(selfIndexValue(0), consumedIndexValueByIndex($0, 0))           // same holderID
)

// $0 selfUnlockParameters
func _referencedIndex : if( isZero(len($0)), 0xff, byte($0,0))

// $0 — holderID (32 bytes) that must authorise the spend. Supplied by the
// public 0-arg `sigLock` wrapper from the index-value tuple at position 0,
// and by the conditional locks that delegate here (sendWithDeadline,
// tagAlong, delegateLock, htlc) from wherever their own party lives.
//
// Consumed side is the plain signer check: the tx signature's holder must be
// $0. The reference-unlock shortcut is deliberately NOT here — see
// `unlockedByReference` above and the `sigLock` wrapper below.
func _sigLock: and(
	require(equal(selfBlockIndex, lockConstraintIndex), !!!locks_must_be_at_lockConstraintIndex),
	or(
		and(
			selfIsProducedOutput,
			equal(len($0), u64/32),
		    selfEnforceZeroAmountsInNonChainedOutput
		),
		and(
			selfIsConsumedOutput,
			equal($0, txHolderID(txSignatureData))
		)
	)
)

// public 0-arg lock — holderID comes from the index-value tuple.
//
// Unlock data is 1 byte: either the index of a previous sigLock input with a
// strictly smaller index (reference unlock — same holder required), or 0xff
// to say the holderID matches the tx signature's holder. The reference path
// sits here so the conditional locks delegating to `_sigLock` get the
// signature check alone.
func sigLock : or(
	and(
		selfIsConsumedOutput,
		equal(selfBlockIndex, lockConstraintIndex),
		unlockedByReference(_referencedIndex(selfUnlockParameters))
	),
	_sigLock(selfIndexValue(0))
)
